Rett syndrome
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Regression of acquired skills, Loss of purposeful hand skills, Stereotypic hand wringing, Acquired microcephaly

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-042
Reason for Visit: Acute respiratory distress secondary to severe, prolonged breath-holding spells.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 6-year-old female with Rett syndrome experiencing an acute, relentless cycle of extreme hyperventilation followed by prolonged breath-holding, leading to transient perioral cyanosis and near-syncope over the past 2 hours. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the severe risk of hypoxic seizure or cardiac arrhythmia, requiring immediate clinical evaluation and behavioral/pharmacological intervention to break the autonomic dysregulation cycle.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: She is breathing incredibly fast, then locking her breath until her lips turn blue and she almost passes out.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 2-hour history of severe breathing irregularities during wakefulness. The patient has a known history of a period of normal early development followed by a profound regression of acquired language and motor skills. Currently, the patient exhibits complete loss of purposeful hand skills and continuous stereotypic hand wringing. This evening, an alteration in her routine triggered severe anxiety, initiating an unrelenting cycle of alternating hyperventilation and breath-holding. The breath-holding spells are lasting up to 45 seconds, causing significant hypoxia and near-syncope, followed by compensatory, gasping hyperventilation.
Functional Status: Ambulant with an ataxic wide-based gait; totally dependent for all ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of trauma from falls during syncopal episodes; severe parental exhaustion and distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 mmHg | HR: 140 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 40 bpm (During hyperventilation phase) | SpO2: Fluctuating 85% - 98% | Wt: 18.0 Kg
General: Patient is highly agitated, exhibiting continuous stereotypic hand washing/wringing movements, and cycling through profound respiratory dysrhythmia.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals acquired microcephaly and severe expressive and receptive language impairment. Gait is not formally tested due to agitation, but baseline ataxia is noted. Respiratory examination reveals clear lung fields with no wheeze or crackles; the abnormal breathing is purely centrally mediated. During the breath-holding phase, profound bradycardia (HR dropping to 60 bpm) and transient cyanosis (SpO2 85%) are observed, recovering rapidly once breathing resumes. Cardiovascular exam shows no structural murmurs.
Environmental Observations: The room is brightly lit and a television is playing loudly, exacerbating sensory overload.
Medication Reconciliation: Routine medications administered. No PRN anxiolytics available at home.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Rett syndrome (complicated by acute severe autonomic respiratory dysregulation) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Acute Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of expiratory wheeze, the clear cyclical nature of the breathing (hyperventilation/apnoea), and normal SpO2 during the hyperventilation phase.

Seizure Activity (Complex Partial) - Ruled out/unlikely as the breath-holding is provoked by agitation, consciousness is largely preserved between spells, and there is no rhythmic clonic motor activity.

Upper Airway Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of inspiratory stridor and the complete resolution of distress during the hyperventilation phases.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a relentless hyperventilation and breath-holding cycle required urgent medical intervention. The objective findings of fluctuating hypoxia, transient bradycardia, and severe agitation confirmed profound autonomic dysregulation characteristic of Rett syndrome, exacerbated by sensory overload. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the continuous hypoxic insults posed an immediate risk for a secondary generalized seizure or fatal cardiac arrhythmia. Immediate, in-person clinical management to modify the sensory environment and provide acute anxiolysis was mandatory to break the respiratory cycle.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately dimmed the room lights, turned off all electronic devices, and instructed the parents to enforce a strict, low-stimulation environment. Administered a low-dose oral Clonazepam drop to provide targeted central anxiolysis and break the autonomic hyper-arousal cycle.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you gently massage her hands when the hand-wringing accelerates, you provide calming proprioceptive input; therefore, initiating this tactile pressure early prevents the escalation into the breathing crisis. When you enforce a strictly quiet, dark environment the moment hyperventilation begins, you remove the sensory triggers; relying on this environmental control immediately aborts the breath-holding reflex.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to the complex pediatric neurology team to formalize a home PRN anxiolytic plan.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a padded floor mat in the living area to prevent injury during near-syncopal drops.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow to monitor baseline breathing and assess the efficacy of the environmental changes.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately if a breath-holding spell lasts longer than 60 seconds, if the patient loses consciousness entirely and begins convulsing, or if she sustains a head injury from a fall.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The respiratory dysrhythmia is centrally driven by the genetic mutation and cannot be cured, only managed through environmental and pharmacological suppression.
Clinical Course: The clonazepam will induce significant sedation and regularize the breathing pattern within 45 minutes. The breathing abnormalities will likely return to their milder baseline state tomorrow.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the patient's breathing continuously while she sleeps tonight. Seek urgent medical care if she is excessively difficult to rouse or if her resting breathing rate drops below 12 breaths per minute.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Her brain's control center for breathing and anxiety is misfiring, causing her to hyperventilate and then forget to breathe. The loud environment pushed this misfiring into a severe crisis. The medication acts as a chemical brake to calm the brain stem down, allowing her natural, automatic breathing rhythm to take over again.
Actionable Steps: Keep the house entirely quiet and dim tonight. Perform gentle hand massage during periods of agitation. Ensure she sleeps in a safe, low-to-the-ground position.

